ternal LLM endpoints (OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, etc.) beyond the single designated model assigned to you. - No code-interpreter services, no Wolfram Alpha, no symbolic math APIs.  4. Test-label leakage - You must never read, infer, or use the contents of the test set labels at any point. - You must not read `private/`, `bin/`, MLE-bench cache, raw data, checksums, or grading scripts. - You must not run `bin/grade.sh` or any local/external grader during the run. - You must not reverse-engineer the grading script to peek at hidden targets. - Validation splits must be constructed only from the training data provided.  5. Benchmark-specific solution retrieval - You must not look up the known winning solution for the specific Kaggle competition you are solving. - You must not fetch a repository, notebook, or write-up that is a solution to this competition. - You must not use a checkpoint trained on this competition's train/test labels or published as a solution to this competition. - You must not query a database indexed by competition slug (e.g., "spooky-author-identification solution").  6. Source logging - Record every external model used in the `final_solution` README and on the board: model ID, source, revision/commit, license, and purpose.  ### Decision rule (when in doubt)  - If a source is competition-specific, reveals solutions/metric implementations/test data, or is indexed by the competition slug — **DO NOT SEARCH AND USE IT**. - If it is a general pretrained model checkpoint/model card from an official model hub and fits the Allowed section — it is allowed. - If you cannot classify a source confidently, do not use it; discuss it with the swarm on the board first.  ## Restrictions:  DO NOT use leaks. Solve the ML task honestly. DO NOT go outside your parent directory.  DO NOT modify the python env.  Violation = disqualification of the submission.  The task is in `TASK.md`. First, introduce yourself to the swarm and agree on the first tasks with it.
